WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:06.710
The explosion of medical
knowledge and technology

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.560
has led to a dramatic
increase in specialization

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.800
of medical care,
provider's insights.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.160
One aspect of this is that
care for complex problems

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.440
has become increasingly
regionalized.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.080
One downside of
this centralization

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.350
is that the relative
ability to properly treat

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:28.490
some classes of patients at
non-centers has declined.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.100
A prime example of
this specialization

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.410
is neonatal care.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.890
Newborn medicine has
become highly specialized.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:40.280
One problem is that many
small hospitals, which

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.310
are perfectly well-qualified
to deliver babies,

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:47.720
are not set up to properly
care for seriously ill infants.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:49.850
This has led to a
dramatic increase

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.280
in inter-hospital transport.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:57.320
Unfortunately, sick babies can
decline astonishingly quickly.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.040
The goal of our system is to
provide continuous real time

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.580
medical tele-presence that
will allow experts at the best

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.190
neonatal care facilities
to participate

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.890
in the care of babies,
both while the baby is

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.140
at the hospital in
which it was born

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:15.050
and, should transport
be necessary,

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:16.970
during that critical
period of time when

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:19.460
the baby is between hospitals.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.040
We believe that
deployment of this system

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.190
will lead to better patient
outcomes, improved care

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:30.440
while at the community hospital,
improved care during transport,

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:34.100
and better preparation
at the receiving hospital

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.070
so that urgent care can
be provided immediately

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:38.480
upon arrival.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.050
Our system has three components,
an instrumented environment

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:45.250
in which the baby can be
cared for and transported,

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.360
a communication
subsystem that provides

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.390
a bi-directional,
real-time video link

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:53.120
between regional
neonatal care centers,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.940
community hospitals,
and the vehicles used

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.330
to transport babies,
and an application that

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:02.990
facilitates collaboration
amongst those in charge

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.820
of caring for the infant.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.520
The most technologically
challenging component

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.970
of the system to build was
the communication subsystem.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:14.120
It is built on top of the
commercial cellular data

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.530
networks.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:19.880
Using the cellular data networks
poses a number of challenges.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.790
These networks provide
limited bandwidth,

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.670
they suffer from a
high packet loss rate,

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.550
and they are
notoriously unstable.

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:31.730
Our solution is to
multiplex the streams

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:36.410
over multiple cellular channels
and exploit carrier diversity.

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:39.140
Here is a video of
one of our test runs.

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:42.260
The first thing we see is
the transport team loading

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.260
the isolate into the truck.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.850
Here is a physician from
Children's Hospital sitting

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:49.310
at the application that
runs at the hospital.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:52.220
There are three cameras, two
focused on the baby and one

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:54.470
on the vital signs monitor.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:58.760
The doctors can switch back
and forth among these cameras.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.040
There is unfortunately
a slight delay

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.110
when the camera is changed to
allow the buffers to refill

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:08.750
so that the video provided
can continue to be smooth.

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:11.990
The doctor is also visible
on the user interface

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:14.330
in the ambulance,
which allows the doctor

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:18.260
to demonstrate procedures that
he or she might wish the team

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:20.000
in the ambulance to follow.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.580
Here's a video from
a different test run.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.040
This baby is approximately
two months old

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.930
and considerably larger
than those babies

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:29.510
we expect to be transported.

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:32.810
Notice, however, that
the motion, as perceived

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:37.100
by the doctor, is quite normal
and the resolution perfectly

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.590
adequate for
diagnostic purposes.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:42.740
Frequently, the physicians
will observe something

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:46.140
that they wish to refer
to another physician.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.610
They can save video.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.740
Typically, what
the doctor will do

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.430
is want to save video something
he or she has previously seen,

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:57.720
so the doctor is allowed to
save the previous minute or two

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.250
minutes or five
minutes of video.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:04.020
Video is saved on a web server
at the hospital, which can then

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:08.190
be accessed by anyone with
access to the hospital's

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:09.210
network.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:12.810
It's also possible
to zoom in or out.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:14.940
Doctors are likely
to do this to inspect

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:18.779
wounds or skin lesions,
perhaps the eyes, things

00:04:18.779 --> 00:04:20.100
of that nature.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.079
One of the more popular
features amongst

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:25.740
the doctors is the
ability to take a snapshot

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.050
and email that snapshot
to a collaborator

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.730
for a second opinion.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.550
In our tests, we
discovered that our system

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.970
was considerably more reliable
than cellular telephone

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.680
communications.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.350
We also discovered
that the level of noise

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.890
in the ambulance, particularly
when the respirator was on,

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.710
was sufficiently high that
oral communication could

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:50.730
be a challenge, and the
doctors and the transport team

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.610
began to rely increasingly
on the instant messenger

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.250
facility for communications.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.050
In conjunction with
our collaborators

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.120
at Boston's Children's
Hospital, we

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.090
have done several
simulated transport tests.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:05.670
The physicians found
the system easy to use

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.740
and postulated that
it now provides

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:12.150
all the facilities they need to
make an impact on patient care.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:16.380
Premature births account
for 75% of neonatal deaths

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.620
and 50% of
neurological disorders.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.440
Despite these dire
numbers, it is

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:26.310
important to remember that
even for very premature babies,

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:30.210
excellent outcomes are
possible with the proper care.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.790
Our work should help
more babies receive

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:36.530
care that will lead
to excellent outcomes.

